css='body{margin:0;background:#f5f0e8;color:#28232c;font:18px/1.65 Georgia,serif}main{max-width:1180px;margin:auto;padding:38px 28px}h1{font-size:48px;line-height:1.1}p{max-width:80ch}a{color:#705079}nav{display:flex;gap:20px;flex-wrap:wrap}img{width:100%;height:auto;display:block}figure{margin:28px 0}figcaption,small{font-size:15px}figcaption{padding:10px 0}.pair{display:grid;grid-template-columns:1fr 1fr;gap:18px}.pair figure{margin:0}details{border:1px solid #cdc5bd;padding:16px;margin:18px 0}summary{cursor:pointer;font-weight:bold}.diffwrap{overflow:auto}table{width:100%;border-collapse:collapse}td,th{padding:10px;vertical-align:top;border-bottom:1px solid #ccc}.diff{font:12px/1.45 monospace}.diff td{padding:4px}.diff_header{background:#e3ddd4}.diff_add{background:#d3e8d5}.diff_sub{background:#f1d3d2}.diff_chg{background:#f3e6ae}@media(max-width:700px){h1{font-size:34px}.pair{grid-template-columns:1fr}}'
page='''<!doctype html><html lang="en"><meta charset="utf-8"><meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width,initial-scale=1"><title>Forces — which body hears it?</title><style>'''+css+'''</style><main><p>Ada Research · 25 September 2026</p><h1>Which body hears it?</h1><p>A cube, a stick request and a person’s physical steps can share one coloured strip without receiving the same instruction. Forces becomes strongest where we can discover that difference by moving through it.</p><nav><a href="#reading">Exact text differences</a><a href="/compose/sources?map=Vectors_Act4a_KineticForces&amp;doc=final">Read the drag passage</a><a href="/compose/sources?map=Vectors_Act5_ForceAsPlace&amp;doc=final">Read the crossing</a><a href="/book-tasks/review?seq=forces">Review comments</a><a href="DECISIONS.md">Decisions and evidence</a></nav><figure><img src="drag/inside-the-passage.png" alt="Standing inside the coloured drag passage, looking along it into the museum"><figcaption>The cubes cross the strips while the visitor’s passage runs through them. Their apparent resistance has different causes.</figcaption></figure><h2>A small change of address</h2><p>The drag paragraph now speaks to the visitor holding a controller. It keeps the distinction between the stick request, a physical drag force and the steps taken in the room outside the headset. This distinction already belongs to the work; the text no longer makes VR an aside to the desktop.</p><h2>What the plank keeps</h2><figure><img src="place/two-crossings.png" alt="The central plank through the outlined carrying field inside the museum"><figcaption>The solid route remains beside the rule that can carry a body. This photograph is taken from the standing approach.</figcaption></figure><p>The field releases its receiver at the boundary. In the air, motion continues under ordinary gravity; the default XR body regains stick steering when its feet reach support. The revised paragraph follows that sequence. Keeping the plank makes both routes available to compare.</p><h2>Even stillness has a threshold</h2><p>The old cube could retain a tiny motion arrow after stopping. A small movement followed by zero left the old drawing in place. The lesson now clears it before the redraw tolerance can preserve it, and clears the collapsed component marks too.</p><p>At the existing display scale, movements at or below four centimetres per second count as stillness. The text names the choice without turning the encounter into a calibration manual: “Even stillness has a threshold in this picture.” The legacy cube display elsewhere is retained.</p><details><summary>The recorded before-and-after</summary><table><thead><tr><th>Supplied sequence</th><th>Before</th><th>After</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>0.03 m/s, then zero</td><td>About 0.015 m of arrow remained</td><td>Arrow and collapsed component geometry cleared</td></tr><tr><td>Movement resumes at 0.041 m/s</td><td>—</td><td>Arrow returns</td></tr></tbody></table><p><a href="place-before/report.json">Baseline evidence</a> · <a href="place/report.json">Corrected native run</a> · <a href="diffs/commons/artifacts/force_cube/force_cube.gd.diff">Exact artifact change</a>. These are supplied position samples, not tracked controller measurements.</p></details><h2>The joins stay short</h2><p>The opening remembers the particles in Flow Field. Choreography now leads into Gravity by asking whether a centre can answer another body. The final hall receives a title, acceleration receives its unit, and the yaw passage distinguishes a vertical field from the readout’s small idle region. Gravity’s softening gets a name and a linked footnote.</p><p>Ten review comments addressed. Five final.md chapters changed; eight retained. The current thirteen-hall Forces capture is refreshed. The maps, artifact roles and companion texts remain intact. Two native museum photographs enter the book.</p><details><summary>Checks and limits</summary><p><a href="drag/report.json">10 Kinetic Forces checks</a> · <a href="place/report.json">31 Force as Place checks</a>. Both final runs passed and exited 0, with no GDScript errors. The XR handoff uses the production provider with a nontracked body fixture and supplied contact response. This checks its contract, not a person’s headset crossing.</p><p>Actual controller jitter, readability, reach and comfort still need headset experience. Existing engine warnings remain; the drag run also reports renderer and ObjectDB leaks at exit. No claim of a clean shutdown or Quest performance. No live museum bake was replaced.</p><p><a href="verification.json">Publication and preservation checks</a> · <a href="figure-provenance.json">Unmodified image provenance</a> · <a href="queue-verification.json">Review queue preservation</a>.</p></details><h2 id="reading">Exact changes in final.md</h2>'''+''.join(parts)+'''<h2>Comment by comment</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Review item</th><th>Decision</th></tr></thead><tbody>'''+''.join(rows)+'''</tbody></table></main></html>'''
(o/'index.html').write_text(page,encoding='utf-8');print('Published:',len(checks),'scoped checks passed; 10 decisions; 2 native figures; 1 artifact fix.')
